  "worked_solution": "First, we decompose all of the terms into their various factors. The $2(x-1)$ in the numerator becomes $2\\cdot (x-1),$ and the $2(x+1)$ in the denominator becomes $2\\cdot (x+1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x-1)}{2(x+1)}=\\frac{2\\cdot (x-1)}{2\\cdot (x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we cancel out all the pairs of common factors in the numerator and denominator. In this case, there is only one pair of $2$'s.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\cdot (x-1)}{2\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (x-1)}{\\cancel{2}\\cdot (x+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)}{(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to $\\frac{x-1}{x+1}.$",
